<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Carillon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1758</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Ticonderoga (1758) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Carillon was fought on 8 July 1758 at Fort Carillon, later Ticonderoga, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-champlain">Lake Champlain</a>. About 3,600 French troops under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-joseph-de-montcalm">Marquis de Montcalm</a>, behind a log entrenchment, repulsed repeated frontal attacks by a British army of some 16,000 under James Abercrombie, inflicting about 2,000 casualties. It was the greatest French victory of the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-carillon.jpg" alt="Battle of Carillon" width="320" height="288" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Alexander Ogden (1854-1936) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Victory_of_Montcalms_Troops_at_Carillon_by_Henry_Alexander_Ogden.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
